 "views": [
  {
   "mode": "process",
   "beat": 1,
   "title": "Why a straight tube cannot stay straight",
   "ops": [
    {
     "op": "HIDE_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "*"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "sinus_a"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "atrium_a"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "ventricle_a"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "bulbus_a"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "truncus_a"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "meso_a"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "veins_c"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "arches_a"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "peri_a"
    },
    {
     "op": "ROTATE_TO_VIEW",
     "view": "anterior"
    }
   ],
   "narration": "By day twenty-three the heart tube is tied at both ends — arteries above, veins below — and it is elongating faster than the pericardial cavity around it. Cells added at both poles from the secondary heart field keep lengthening it. A tube that is growing, held at both ends and running out of room can only do one thing, and it bends. Looping is mechanics before it is genetics; the genes decide which way, not whether.\n\nStart with the straight tube at day twenty-three. It is anchored above by the arch arteries and below by the veins, its dorsal mesocardium has broken down in the middle, and the secondary heart field keeps adding cells at both poles so it lengthens inside a pericardial cavity that does not lengthen with it. Held at both ends, free in the middle, growing. It bends."
  },
  {
   "mode": "process",
   "beat": 2,
   "title": "The dorsal mesocardium has already broken down",
   "ops": [
    {
     "op": "HIDE_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "*"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "sinus_a"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "atrium_a"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "ventricle_a"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "bulbus_a"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "truncus_a"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "veins_c"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "arches_a"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "meso_a"
    },
    {
     "op": "ROTATE_TO_VIEW",
     "view": "lateral"
    },
    {
     "op": "HIGHLIGHT_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "meso_a",
     "intensity": 0.7
    }
   ],
   "narration": "The middle of the dorsal mesocardium has already given way, so the tube hangs free between its two fixed ends. That is what makes bending possible at all. The gap it leaves behind is the transverse pericardial sinus, which is why in the adult you can pass a finger behind the aorta and pulmonary trunk and in front of the atria."
  },
  {
   "mode": "process",
   "beat": 3,
   "title": "Movement one — the bulbus swings ventrally, caudally and to the RIGHT",
   "ops": [
    {
     "op": "HIDE_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "*"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "sinus_b"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "atrium_b"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "ventricle_b"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "bulbus_b"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
