    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "veins_c"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "arches_b"
    },
    {
     "op": "HIGHLIGHT_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "bulbus_b",
     "intensity": 0.85
    },
    {
     "op": "ROTATE_TO_VIEW",
     "view": "anterior"
    }
   ],
   "narration": "The first movement is the one that names the loop. The bulbus cordis, the cranial half of the tube, swings forwards, downwards and to the right. That is the D-loop, D for dextro, and it is the normal direction in every healthy human heart. Say it as one sentence you can repeat under exam pressure: the bulbus goes ventral, caudal and right."
  },
  {
   "mode": "process",
   "beat": 4,
   "title": "Movement two — the ventricle swings dorsally and to the LEFT",
   "ops": [
    {
     "op": "HIDE_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "*"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "sinus_b"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "atrium_b"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "ventricle_b"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "bulbus_b"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "truncus_b"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "veins_c"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "arches_b"
    },
    {
     "op": "HIGHLIGHT_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "ventricle_b",
     "intensity": 0.85
    }
   ],
   "narration": "The second movement is the mirror of the first. The primitive ventricle moves backwards and to the left. Bulbus to the right, ventricle to the left, and the two now sit side by side rather than one behind the other. The groove between them outside is the bulboventricular sulcus, and the opening between them inside is the primary interventricular foramen — the hole the muscular septum will later grow up towards."
  },
  {
   "mode": "process",
   "beat": 5,
   "title": "Movement three — the atria climb up behind",
   "ops": [
    {
     "op": "HIDE_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "*"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "sinus_c"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "atrium_c"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "ventricle_c"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "bulbus_c"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "truncus_c"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "veins_c"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "arches_c"
    },
    {
     "op": "HIGHLIGHT_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "atrium_c",
     "intensity": 0.85
    },
    {
     "op": "HIGHLIGHT_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "sinus_c",
     "intensity": 0.85
    },
    {
     "op": "ROTATE_TO_VIEW",
     "view": "lateral"
    }
   ],
   "narration": "The third movement carries the atrium and the sinus venosus backwards and upwards, so the inflow end comes to lie behind and above the ventricles instead of below them. This is the movement that produces the adult arrangement: atria posterior and superior, ventricles anterior and inferior, and the great vessels rising between the two atria in front. Every relation you learn in the adult mediastinum was set here."
  },
  {
   "mode": "process",
   "beat": 6,
   "title": "What the loop leaves you with",
   "ops": [
    {
     "op": "HIDE_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "*"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "sinus_c"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "atrium_c"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "ventricle_c"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "bulbus_c"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "truncus_c"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "veins_c"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "arches_c"
    },
    {
     "op": "ROTATE_TO_VIEW",
     "view": "anterior"
    }
   ],
   "narration": "By about day twenty-eight the tube has become an S. The two future ventricles lie side by side, the two future atria lie side by side behind and above them, the atrioventricular canal joins the atrial half to the ventricular half, and the outflow tract runs up in the midline between the atria. Nothing has been divided yet — this is one continuous lumen. But the pieces are now in the right places relative to each other.\n\nThree sentences, in this order. One: the bulbus cordis swings ventrally, caudally and to the right — that is the D-loop. Two: the primitive ventricle moves dorsally and to the left, so the two future ventricles now lie side by side. Three: the atrium and sinus venosus move dorsally and cranially, so the inflow ends up behind and above the outflow. If you can say those three lines you can draw the loop from memory."
  },
  {
   "mode": "process",
   "beat": 7,
   "title": "Looping is the setup for septation",
   "ops": [
    {
     "op": "HIDE_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "*"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "sinus_c"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "atrium_c"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "ventricle_c"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "bulbus_c"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "truncus_c"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "veins_c"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "arches_c"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "endo_c"
    },
    {
     "op": "CROSS_SECTION",
     "target": "ventricle_c",
     "axis": "x"
    }
   ],
   "narration": "Understand looping as the step that brings each part alongside the part it must be joined to. The septa built next are all short walls between structures that looping has already parked next to one another. That is why a looping or alignment error shows up later as a connection fault rather than a hole: the great arteries coming off the wrong ventricle, or both off the right. If the pieces are put in the wrong place, no amount of correct wall-building fixes it.\n\nBy day twenty-eight it is an S-shaped heart with a single continuous lumen. Ventricles side by side in front and below, atria side by side behind and above, joined through the atrioventricular canal, with the outflow tract climbing between the two atria. Cut it across and check yourself against the segment colours from the tube scene — nothing has been added or lost, the same five segments have simply been repositioned. That repositioning is the whole purpose of the step, because septation is only short walls between parts that are already correctly parked."
  },
  {
   "mode": "process",
   "beat": 8,
   "title": "L-loop — the tube turns the wrong way",
   "ops": [
    {
     "op": "HIDE_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "*"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "d_sinus"
    },
    {
