     "target": "d_atrium"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "d_ventricle"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "d_bulbus"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "d_truncus"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "d_veins"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "d_arches"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "l_sinus"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "l_atrium"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "l_ventricle"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "l_bulbus"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "l_truncus"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "l_veins"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "l_arches"
    },
    {
     "op": "COMPARE_STRUCTURES",
     "targets": [
      "d_sinus",
      "d_atrium",
      "d_ventricle",
      "d_bulbus",
      "d_truncus",
      "l_sinus",
      "l_atrium",
      "l_ventricle",
      "l_bulbus",
      "l_truncus"
     ]
    },
    {
     "op": "ROTATE_TO_VIEW",
     "view": "anterior"
    }
   ],
   "narration": "If the tube loops to the left instead of the right, the ventricles end up inverted — the morphological right ventricle on the left and the left ventricle on the right. When the great arteries are also transposed, the two errors cancel each other for flow and the child is pink: congenitally corrected transposition. Do not read 'corrected' as 'normal'. The right ventricle is pumping to the body for a lifetime, and it fails eventually, with conduction tissue that is abnormally placed and prone to block."
  },
  {
   "mode": "process",
   "beat": 9,
   "title": "Mirror-image anatomy — and the difference from dextroposition",
   "ops": [
    {
     "op": "HIDE_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "*"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "sinus_l"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "atrium_l"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "ventricle_l"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "bulbus_l"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "truncus_l"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "veins_l"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "arches_l"
    },
    {
     "op": "SHOW_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "midline_c"
    },
    {
     "op": "ROTATE_TO_VIEW",
     "view": "anterior"
    },
    {
     "op": "HIGHLIGHT_STRUCTURE",
     "target": "midline_c",
     "intensity": 0.6
    }
   ],
   "narration": "Dextrocardia means the heart is built pointing to the right; dextroposition means a normal heart has been pushed to the right by something else, such as a right lung agenesis or a left tension pneumothorax. The distinction is the first thing to make on the film. When dextrocardia comes with full situs inversus the internal relations are a clean mirror image and the heart is usually otherwise normal. Isolated dextrocardia, with the abdominal organs in the usual place, carries a high rate of associated defects. About a quarter of situs inversus cases are Kartagener syndrome, where immotile cilia give bronchiectasis, chronic sinusitis and infertility alongside the mirrored anatomy — one ciliary defect, four findings.\n\nThree ways this goes wrong. Loop the other way and the ventricles are inverted: congenitally corrected transposition, pink at birth but with a right ventricle doing systemic work for life. Reverse the whole plan and you get situs inversus with dextrocardia, usually a normal heart, and about a quarter of the time Kartagener syndrome. Fail to choose a side at all and you get isomerism — two right sides with asplenia and severe cardiac disease, or two left sides with polysplenia and conduction problems. Then answer any case in the same three steps: situs, loop, connections."
  }
 ],
